blocks.en:
\begin{enumerate}
\item In Chapter~\ref{chap:introduction}, we noted that any twice differentiable function of the form $u(x,t)=F(x+t)+G(x-t)$ is a solution of the wave equation $u_{tt}=u_{xx}$. Clearly, it seems natural to call $u$ a "generalized" solution even if $F$ and $G$ are not twice differentiable. A natural question is what meaning can be given to $u_{tt}$ and $u_{xx}$ in this case; obviously, they cannot be "functions" in the usual sense. The same question arises for the shock solutions of hyperbolic conservation laws which we discussed in Chapter~\ref{chap:conservation-laws-shocks}.
\item Consider the ODE initial-value problem
\begin{equation}\label{eq:impulse-ode-initial-value-problem}
u'(t)=f_\epsilon(t),\ u(0)=0,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}\label{eq:unit-impulse-approximating-pulse}
f_\epsilon(t)=\begin{cases}\dfrac1\epsilon&1<t<1+\epsilon\\0&\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Obviously, the solution is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:impulse-ode-piecewise-solution}
u(t)=\begin{cases}0&0\leq t\leq1\\\dfrac{t-1}{\epsilon}&1\leq t\leq1+\epsilon\\1&t\geq1+\epsilon.\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Note that the limit of $u$ as $\epsilon\to0$ exists; it is a step function. The function $f_\epsilon$ has unit integral; it is supported on shorter and shorter time intervals as $\epsilon$ tends to zero. It would be natural to regard the "limit" of $f_\epsilon$ as an instantaneous unit impulse. The question arises what meaning can be given to this limit and in what sense the differential equation holds in the limit. Similar questions arise in many physical problems involving idealized point singularities: the electric field of a point charge, light emitted by a point source, etc.
\item In Chapter~\ref{chap:introduction}, we outlined the solution of Dirichlet\textquotesingle s problem by minimizing the integral $\int_\Omega|\nabla u|^2\,\mathrm{d}\bm{x}$. A fundamental ingredient in turning these ideas into a rigorous theory is obviously the definition of a class of functions for which the integral is finite; the square root of the integral naturally defines a norm on this space of functions. It turns out that $C^1(\overline\Omega)$ is too restrictive; it is not a complete metric space in the norm defined by the integral. It is natural to consider the completion; this leads to functions for which $\nabla u$ does not exist in the sense of the classical definition as a pointwise limit of difference quotients.
\item The Fourier transform is a natural tool for dealing with PDEs with constant coefficients posed on all of space. However, the class of functions for which the Fourier integral exists in the conventional sense is rather restrictive; in particular, such functions must be integrable at infinity. Clearly, it would be useful to have a notion of the Fourier transform for functions which do not satisfy such a restriction, e.g., constant functions.
\end{enumerate}

blocks.zh:
\begin{enumerate}
\item 在 Chapter~\ref{chap:introduction} 中指出, 形如 $u(x,t)=F(x+t)+G(x-t)$ 的任意二次可微函数都是波动方程 $u_{tt}=u_{xx}$ 的解. 显然, 即使 $F$ 和 $G$ 并非二次可微, 似乎也自然应将 $u$ 称为一个"广义"解. 一个自然的问题是, 此时能赋予 $u_{tt}$ 和 $u_{xx}$ 什么意义; 显然, 它们不能是通常意义下的"函数". 对 Chapter~\ref{chap:conservation-laws-shocks} 中讨论过的双曲守恒律的激波解, 也出现同样的问题.
\item 考虑常微分方程初值问题
\begin{equation}\label{eq:impulse-ode-initial-value-problem}
u'(t)=f_\epsilon(t),\ u(0)=0,
\end{equation}
其中
\begin{equation}\label{eq:unit-impulse-approximating-pulse}
f_\epsilon(t)=\begin{cases}\dfrac1\epsilon&1<t<1+\epsilon\\0&\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\end{equation}
显然, 解为
\begin{equation}\label{eq:impulse-ode-piecewise-solution}
u(t)=\begin{cases}0&0\leq t\leq1\\\dfrac{t-1}{\epsilon}&1\leq t\leq1+\epsilon\\1&t\geq1+\epsilon.\end{cases}
\end{equation}
注意, 当 $\epsilon\to0$ 时, $u$ 的极限存在; 它是一个阶梯函数. 函数 $f_\epsilon$ 的积分为一; 当 $\epsilon$ 趋于零时, 它的支集位于越来越短的时间区间内. 将 $f_\epsilon$ 的"极限"视为瞬时单位脉冲是自然的. 问题是能赋予这一极限什么意义, 以及微分方程在极限下以何种意义成立. 许多涉及理想化点奇性的物理问题中也有类似的问题: 点电荷的电场, 点光源发出的光, 等等.
\item 在 Chapter~\ref{chap:introduction} 中概述了通过极小化积分 $\int_\Omega|\nabla u|^2\,\mathrm{d}\bm{x}$ 来求解 Dirichlet 问题. 要将这些想法变成严格的理论, 一个基本要素显然是定义一类使该积分有限的函数; 积分的平方根自然地在这一函数空间上定义一个范数. 事实表明, $C^1(\overline\Omega)$ 限制过强; 在该积分所定义的范数下, 它不是完备度量空间. 自然应考虑其完备化; 这将引出一些函数, 它们的 $\nabla u$ 在差商逐点极限这一经典定义的意义下并不存在.
\item Fourier 变换是处理全空间上常系数偏微分方程的自然工具. 然而, 在通常意义下 Fourier 积分存在的函数类限制颇强; 特别地, 这些函数在无穷远处必须可积. 显然, 对不满足这一限制的函数, 例如常函数, 若能有 Fourier 变换的概念, 将很有用.
\end{enumerate}

blocks.en:
The idea behind generalized functions is roughly this: Given a continuous function $f(\bm{x})$ on $\Omega$, we can define a linear mapping
\begin{equation}\label{eq:function-induced-test-functional}
\phi\mapsto\int_\Omega f(\bm{x})\phi(\bm{x})\,\mathrm{d}\bm{x}
\end{equation}
from a suitable class of functions (which will be called test functions) into $\mathbb{R}$. We shall see that this mapping has certain continuity properties. A generalized function is then defined to be a linear mapping on the test functions with these same continuity properties.

blocks.zh:
广义函数背后的想法大致如下: 给定 $\Omega$ 上的连续函数 $f(\bm{x})$, 可以定义线性映射
\begin{equation}\label{eq:function-induced-test-functional}
\phi\mapsto\int_\Omega f(\bm{x})\phi(\bm{x})\,\mathrm{d}\bm{x}
\end{equation}
将某一适当函数类 (称为试验函数) 映入 $\mathbb{R}$. 将看到, 这一映射具有某些连续性性质. 于是将广义函数定义为试验函数上具有相同连续性性质的线性映射.

blocks.en:
Since we intend to use generalized functions to study differential equations, a key question is: how do we define derivatives of such functions? The answer is: by using integration by parts. We shall require $\phi$ to vanish near $\partial\Omega$, so the derivative $\partial f/\partial x_j$ can be defined as the mapping
\begin{equation}\label{eq:distribution-derivative-motivating-pairing}
\phi\mapsto-\int_\Omega f(\bm{x})\dfrac{\partial\phi}{\partial x_j}(\bm{x})\,\mathrm{d}\bm{x}.
\end{equation}
Clearly, this definition requires no differentiability of $f$ in the usual sense; the only differentiability requirement is on $\phi$. We shall therefore choose the test functions to be functions with very nice smoothness properties.

blocks.zh:
由于打算利用广义函数研究微分方程, 一个关键问题是: 如何定义这类函数的导数? 答案是: 利用分部积分. 将要求 $\phi$ 在 $\partial\Omega$ 附近为零, 因而导数 $\partial f/\partial x_j$ 可以定义为映射
\begin{equation}\label{eq:distribution-derivative-motivating-pairing}
\phi\mapsto-\int_\Omega f(\bm{x})\dfrac{\partial\phi}{\partial x_j}(\bm{x})\,\mathrm{d}\bm{x}.
\end{equation}
显然, 这一定义不要求 $f$ 在通常意义下可微; 唯一的可微性要求是针对 $\phi$ 的. 因此, 将选取具有很好的光滑性性质的函数作为试验函数.
